  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Writing Inequalities to Represent Constraints</title>
      <para>Example</para>
      <para>Elena needs to earn at least $450 a week during her summer break to pay for college. She works two jobs. One as a swimming instructor that pays $9 an hour and the other as an intern in a genetics lab for $22.50 per hour. How many hours does Elena need to work at each job to earn at least $450 per week?</para>
      <para>Let x be the number of hours she works teaching swimming and let y be the number of hours she works as an intern.</para>
      <para>Write an inequality that would model this situation.</para>
      <para>She earns $9 per hour at the job as a swimming instructor and $22.50 in the genetics lab. At each job the number of hours multiplied by the hourly wage will give the amount earned at that job.</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_14_05_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Writing Inequalities to Represent Constraints</title>
      <para>A baker sells pies and cakes at the bakery. Each pie sells for $14 and each cake sells for $23. Between all the sales, the baker wants to earn at least $525. How many pies and cakes does the baker need to sell to meet their goal?</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Let \(p\) be the number of pies sold and let c be the number of cakes sold. Write an inequality that would model this situation.</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: You answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para>\(14p\;+\;23c\;\geq\;525\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
